WEBVTT

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:07.810
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.250
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:21.700
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.460
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.940
and some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.540
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd,

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.330
the curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:53.350
Some animals acquired frightening reputations

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.430
almost as soon as they were discovered.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.960
In this episode, we investigate the stories surrounding two...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.890
such creatures the gorilla the vampire bat why did they get such bad reputations and were they

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:23.580
justified statue in the london zoo is of guy the gorilla he was perhaps the zoo's most well-known

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:29.970
resident and became one of the world's most famous gorillas in his prime guy weighed in

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.060
at over 200 kilos his neck as you can see was thicker than the man's waist and he stood five

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:44.700
foot four inches tall over a meter and a half and that was with his knees bent when guy arrived here

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:51.120
in 1940 little was known about gorillas and the reports from africa hinted of a creature that was

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.020
shockingly brutal so it's hardly surprising that people flocked to see this fierce monster for

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.960
themselves. But Guy proved...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.790
to be a gentle giant who won the affection of the public. So how and why did the gorilla gain

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:15.890
this reputation as a fearsome savage? Today we know a lot about gorillas and their way of life.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.290
There are in fact a number of different kinds, some of which live in the lowlands and others

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:28.460
in the mountains. They stay in small family groups and spend much of their days feeding on leaves and

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:28.720
shoots.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:36.550
Many people, including myself, have traveled a long way to meet these close relatives of ours.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:54.760
Remarkably, despite being the largest living ape, the gorilla was one of the last to be described by science.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.970
In 1847, an American missionary...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
naturalist thomas savage was traveling back home from africa when he stopped off to stay with some

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:15.040
friends in the congo friend's house was decorated with african curiosities and one of them caught

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:23.420
his eye a skull but it was not like one that he'd ever seen before in africa he had two huge eye

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:29.780
sockets a crest like a mohawk haircut running from front to back and another transversely

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.600
These are anchor points for huge muscles for the jaw and the neck.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.680
And he knew immediately he was looking at a spectacular new species.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.540
But there's no time to go in search of it.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.350
He frantically negotiated with some African hunters

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.850
and managed to acquire further skulls and bones of the same kind of animal.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.910
When he got back to the States, Savage handed the specimens to an anatomist friend

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.980
who immediately recognized that they

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.070
belonged to some kind of ape. He gave it the scientific name gorilla, a Greek word meaning

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:16.360
wild, hairy people. He then sealed the reputation of the gorilla with the convention of adding the

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:27.000
surname of the person who discovered it. In this case, Thomas Savage. Many people misguidedly

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.980
assumed that the scientific name gorilla...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.410
The savage eye was a description of the nature of this newly found ape.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.140
Though gorillas had somehow remained unknown to science until Victorian times,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.170
other great apes were already quite familiar.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.560
They were all commonly called Orangs,

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.410
after the most famous of them, the Orangutan,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.140
which the Dutch had encountered in Indonesia in the 17th century.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.960
Shortly afterwards, the Portuguese discovered chimpanzees in Africa.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.030
And by the time reports of the gorilla appeared,

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.630
both chimps and orangs had been appearing in circuses

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.010
on the courts of European royalty for over 200 years.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.980
The first gorillas to arrive in Britain were dead specimens.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.780
And unlike these later arrivals, they were often badly preserved.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:37.790
They went on display at the Crystal Palace, and their grotesque appearance was supported by horrific accounts of their nature.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:45.000
One of the early collectors of guerrillas was an American anthropologist called Dushayu.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:53.280
He made numerous expeditions to Africa and returned with tales of terrifying encounters with guerrillas.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.980
In this, his bestseller, Exploration and...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.660
adventure in equatorial africa amongst sensational tales of cannibalism charging buffalo and

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:12.580
tropical fevers is the very first eyewitness account of man meeting male gorillas in their

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:20.860
jungle home he was a sight i think i shall never forget nearly six feet high with immense body

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:28.700
huge chest and great muscular arms with fiercely glaring large deep gray eyes and a hellish

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:29.680
expression of face.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.710
which seemed to me like some nightmare vision,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:37.110
thus stood before us this king of the African forest.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:44.080
To be fair, Chayu did dispel some of the more ridiculous stories and myths about the guerrilla,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.370
but his compelling tales of their fierce nature was just what the public wanted to hear.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:59.980
Chayu's vivid description of the guerrilla in the wild reinforces its image.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.940
as a fearsome beast and confirmed its reputation.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.480
Displays may look fearsome, but in fact,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.360
they're only rarely followed by physical violence.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.300
Duchesne's description may have wowed readers,

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.260
but the scientific establishment

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:27.920
were rather less easy to impress.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:29.970
He was branded...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.840
a bracket, a plagiarist, and a charlatan.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.300
Some suggested he'd never even visited Africa

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.100
and that his ferocious creatures were in fact gentle.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.390
But he had his strongest support right at the top,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.060
Professor Richard Owen, founder of the London Natural History Museum.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.800
Owen was one of the most respected figures of Victorian science,

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.360
but also one of the most widely disliked.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.980
He was vehemently opposed to...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.960
Darwin's theory of evolution, which suggested that apes and humans were closely related.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.300
Dushaiu's description of a ferocious gorilla suited Owen because it seemed to support his

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.640
view that we could not possibly be related to such dreadful monsters.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:23.300
But he could hardly deny the anatomical similarity between gorillas and humans.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:29.960
This illustration from 1855 shows the skeleton of a man and gorilla.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.420
side by side. It was published by Owen himself and makes clear the likeness

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:46.400
between the two species. But Owen was still not willing to accept that man

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.420
could have ape-like ancestors.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:08.390
In 1860, a great debate about evolution and man's place in the natural world took place in this very room in Oxford.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:17.590
Richard Owen presented compelling evidence for the presence of three structures in the human brain that were absent in a gorilla's.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:22.190
According to Owen, this made the descent of man from apes impossible.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.860
As the only anatomist with access to gorilla specimens, he was confident he was on firm ground.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:29.980
But he hadn't come...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.360
on biologist Thomas Henry Huxley.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.350
Huxley, known as Darwin's bulldog,

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.130
was, in his own words,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:41.040
waiting for this opportunity

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.040
to nail that mendacious humbug Owen

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.500
like a kite to a barn door

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.100
and immediately challenged his findings,

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:50.880
vowing to prove him wrong.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:53.080
In the years that followed,

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.060
Huxley doggedly pursued Owen

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.580
and did indeed prove him wrong on all counts.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:59.980
He found all three brains...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.070
structures in the apes and proved that apes were closer to men than to monkeys.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:12.480
Richard Owen had, according to Huxley, been guilty of willful and deliberate falsehood.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:23.150
Owen and Ushayu's misleading descriptions of the guerrilla failed to disprove our relationship

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:23.910
to apes.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.980
On the contrary, they became a turning point in our acceptance that they...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:39.740
are our cousins but sadly the damage to the gorilla's reputation had already been done

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:51.380
when guy arrived in london almost a hundred years after the discovery of gorillas people

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.660
still regarded him as a fearsome and savage beast

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:07.630
It took the next 30 years of Guy's life for a more accurate picture of the gorilla to emerge.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:15.190
Although gorillas can indeed be dangerous when angry or threatened, most of the time they are mild and peaceful creatures.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:20.960
And nowhere is this shown more clearly than in a charming story from Guy's time here at the zoo.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.240
Guy's cage often attracted sparrows that then became trapped inside.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.870
But rather than kill them, Guy would lift the tiny...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.820
birds carefully onto his hand, examine them, and then release them. He was indeed a gentle giant.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:46.320
Over time, thanks to the determination of field researchers like Dian Fossey,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:59.980
people have seen another side to gorillas. By the time I met them, many of us were ready to...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.690
see them not as savages but as animals that are equally suited to their

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:16.740
environment as we are to ours. So now at last the gorilla, which was once labelled

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:28.160
a fearsome beast, has managed to shake off its undeserved reputation. Join me in

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.970
part two where we are...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.530
the life of another creature that has a bad reputation, the vampire bat.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:45.000
Gorillas had a reputation for ferocity which took them a century to dispel.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:50.940
Our second subject, the vampire bat, has also given rise to horrific stories

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.840
and been the inspiration behind tales of evil.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.970
Ants have had a bad reputation for a...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.960
very long time. As creatures of the night, they're connected with dark mysteries and

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:11.530
devilish goings on. But there was never any real evidence to support these claims of their

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:18.270
evil nature. That is, until the conquistadors returned from South America with tales of

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:24.360
giant bats that dropped down on you as you slept and sucked the very blood from your veins.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.230
Tales of vampire bats.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.740
Stories of giant, blood-sucking bats have long been part of the culture of South American people.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:43.140
Images of them with savage fangs are common, and a bat god was associated with death.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:51.290
But it wasn't until the 18th century that a detailed description of a vampire bat was published in Europe,

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.930
and it came from one of its victims.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:59.970
An Englishman by the name of John Gabriel...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.720
Stedman came back from South America with reports of having been bitten by a vampire.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:13.640
He described a bat of monstrous size that sucked the blood of men and cattle when they're fast asleep.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:19.460
And he proudly declared that he'd managed to catch the beast and cut off its head.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:24.360
Stedman's descriptions were detailed, but nonetheless misleading.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:29.910
His drawing shows, in fact, a bat that feeds on nectar and is only a few centimeters.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:42.090
meters long. He had been bitten by a vampire, but he had blamed the wrong bat. Clouded by their own

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:48.190
ideas of what a vampire should look like, early naturalists jumped to all sorts of conclusions

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:54.690
and assumed that it was the biggest and the most ugly that were the bloodsuckers. In fact,

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:59.960
the name vampire was sometimes given to bats that looked the part, but had never so much.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.880
of sniffed blood. These bats, for example, drawn by the 19th century German naturalist

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:12.470
Ernst Haeckel, belong to a group called the leaf-nose bats because of these strange protrusions

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:18.120
around the end of the nose. This gives them a particularly menacing appearance, and some

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:25.450
early naturalists thought that the nose leaf was in fact the mark of a vampire. The leaf-like

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:29.970
object on its nose was thought to be so sharp the bat could use it to puncture.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.470
of victims' skin, and since many bats have such noseleaves,

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:39.090
over 100 species were mistakenly described as vampires.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.220
In fact, the noseleaf is made of nothing more than soft flesh

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:45.260
and couldn't possibly draw blood.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:47.960
It's used for echolocation.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.020
Echolocation works like sonar.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:54.750
The bats produce high-frequency calls

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.820
and use the returning echoes

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:59.980
to build up a mental map of their surroundings, so that...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.610
they're able to find their way in the pitch dark and hunt for prey.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.700
Most bats produce these calls in their throats,

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:12.710
but leaf-nosed bats project them out through their nose in a beam.

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.670
By doing so, they can feed and echolocate at the same time.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.640
So many leaf-nosed bats have been discovered

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.980
that the arrival in Europe of a specimen of another small...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.440
species in 1810 attracted very little attention. It was simply named Desmodus

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:43.540
rotundus on account of it being a little portly. Some 30 years later when Charles

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:48.120
Darwin was traveling around the world aboard the Beagle, he observed Desmodus

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:53.570
feeding in the wild for the first time. He saw it drinking the blood of sleeping

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:59.810
horses and cattle. He had at last identified the true vampire.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:07.110
We know that there are only three species of vampire bats, and they all live in South America.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:12.900
They're totally unique in being the only mammals to feed exclusively on blood.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:16.180
But feeding on blood is not as easy as you might think.

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:18.560
It's actually a pretty challenging diet.

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:23.900
Blood is made up of water and protein and has virtually no fat.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:27.650
So vampires find it hard to get enough energy.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.960
They must consume 50% of...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.260
their own body weight in blood each night, or they'll die within a few days.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:43.400
Under the cover of darkness, the vampire sets out to hunt.

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:52.720
The noseleaf and echolocation help it to home in on its prey.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.160
The bat approaches carefully.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:08.180
Unlike most other bats, it can use its wings as legs and it walks on its elbows.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:20.550
Once near its victim, it uses its nose leaf in another way.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.820
It acts as a heat-seeking device, guiding the bat to the warmth of its prey.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.050
Many livestock have largely replaced wild jungle animals, but even livestock can be dangerous

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:38.330
to a small bat.

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:48.130
The vampire stalks its prey.

00:18:52.690 --> 00:18:53.730
It's close enough.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:58.330
The teeth are so sharp that a nick is all that's needed.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:06.840
Blood from the wound doesn't clot, but continues the flow.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:11.900
And within a quarter of an hour, the bat can drink 40% of its body weight.

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:17.530
That's the equivalent of one of us drinking 50 pints.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:25.960
Having had its fill, it's back to the roost.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.760
a meal every night is not easy, but vampires have come up with a solution to that problem.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:40.830
Those which have been successful share the blood they've drunk with those who had failed

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:47.940
to collect any. Vampires are most likely to share with those they know well from roosting

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:54.650
and grooming together. It's an act of apparent kindness, but the colony as a whole benefits.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:59.970
so it seems that there is another genticide to the use.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:08.080
bats than anyone could have imagined unfortunately just as light was being

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:12.960
shed on the true nature of the vampire an Irish novelist published a book that

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:18.020
would seal their reputation for the foreseeable future Bram Stoker's classic

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:25.970
Dracula leaves little doubt as to where his inspiration came from his story

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:29.890
combined European myths of vampires that come to haunt the living

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:34.460
There's stories of blood-sucking bats from South America.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:40.460
And it's an association with real vampire bats have struggled to shed.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:52.620
More recently, vampire bats have made headlines once again.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:59.620
It's been discovered that their saliva contains a remarkable blood-thinning agent that's been named Dracula.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.860
in and it's proving to be the most successful treatment for stroke victims. How ironic that

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:12.640
a creature we once believed to be a deadly threat may turn out to save human lives in the future.

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:22.520
Maybe it's time we re-evaluated the reputation of the much maligned vampire bat. Vampire bats

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:29.790
and gorillas were long pursued by unfair reputations but while our fear of gorillas has turned into respect

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:33.650
and admiration, the vampire bat, for many of us,

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:36.270
continues to evoke mixed emotions.
